23
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:22,500
Lesson number 11, understanding order flow price rejection.

24
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:27,000
Knowing when the market participants are rejecting price will improve your trading.

25
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:32,800
What you want to know is you want to know when... Other traders are rejecting price.

26
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:36,010
If you're in a position, you're thinking it's

27
00:01:36,010 --> 00:01:38,206
going to go higher, but price is

28
00:01:38,206 --> 00:01:42,599
being rejected, more often than not, the market's not going to go any higher.

29
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:46,700
So you should understand areas where price is being rejected.

30
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:49,500
Think in non-market terms.

31
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:56,000
Sometimes it's easier for people to understand the market when they think of everyday examples.

32
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:58,712
So when you go to the grocery store to buy

33
00:01:58,712 --> 00:02:01,024
food and on your grocery list is you want to

34
00:02:01,024 --> 00:02:03,799
buy some beef, if the price of beef has risen too much,

35
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:05,800
Do you buy the same amount?

36
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:06,100
No.

37
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:06,600
What do you do?

38
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:09,300
You either stop buying it or you buy less.

39
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:14,600
You know, you think of it as you're buying volume and beef slows down until you basically stop buying beef.

40
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:19,700
You know, usually when the price is too high, you will stop buying the beef. You'll reject the price.

41
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:23,100
Another way to think of it is in terms of dating.

42
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:26,900
You ask a pretty girl several times for a phone number and she keeps turning you down.

43
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:27,900
Eventually you give up.

44
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:29,500
The market is the same.

45
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:33,267
Although there may be a heavy passive buyer or a passive seller

46
00:02:33,267 --> 00:02:38,599
at the end's edge of a bar, at the high of a move or the low of a move,

47
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:42,452
the opposing aggressive seller or aggressive buyer will eventually give

48
00:02:42,452 --> 00:02:45,699
up in rejection if they can't move the price any higher.

49
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:50,518
Going back to earlier how I explained in terms of

50
00:02:50,518 --> 00:02:52,840
dating, think about if you've ever asked

51
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:54,499
a girl to get married.

52
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:58,454
You know, sometimes you're very desperate to get

53
00:02:58,454 --> 00:03:00,076
married, you know, just like trading. for

54
00:03:00,076 --> 00:03:01,698
the market to go in your direction.

55
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:04,557
But if the girl doesn't want to marry you, no

56
00:03:04,557 --> 00:03:06,557
matter what you can do, if she

57
00:03:06,557 --> 00:03:09,699
doesn't want to marry you, she doesn't want to marry you.

58
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:11,300
The market is the same way.

59
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:14,821
You want the market to go up, but if price

60
00:03:14,821 --> 00:03:17,318
is being rejected at a level just past

61
00:03:17,318 --> 00:03:19,503
where you got long, you can hope

62
00:03:19,503 --> 00:03:21,688
and wish all you want, but that's

63
00:03:21,688 --> 00:03:24,497
not going to make the market go any higher.

64
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:31,400
So price rejection is important because it answers the trading question, will this level hold or fail?

65
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:35,300
What happens is price gets to a level and cannot follow through.

66
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:38,600
There was no more interest at that level or price levels just behind it.

67
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:45,600
And usually a sure sign is the market doesn't want to be at or around that price level anymore.

68
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:50,900
You come up to a high in the market, and what's going to happen?

69
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:53,004
Either the market's going to

70
00:03:53,004 --> 00:03:54,408
keep going higher, the market's

71
00:03:54,408 --> 00:03:57,498
going to go sideways, or the market's going to sell off.

72
00:03:57,700 --> 00:04:01,100
If price is going to be accepted, price will keep going higher.

73
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:06,300
If the market is sort of indecisive, the market may go sideways.

74
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:09,200
If price is going to be rejected, the market will go lower.

75
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,700
So order flow allows you to see areas where low volume is occurring, which is a sign of price rejection.

76
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:18,800
That's a simple explanation.

77
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:23,053
So imagine as the market's going higher and higher, but

78
00:04:23,053 --> 00:04:25,960
the volume at the highs is getting

79
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:29,698
smaller and smaller, buyers are rejecting those higher prices.

80
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:33,900
So the problem with that expression is this low volume doesn't necessarily mean the price was rejected.

81
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:37,300
Price could have moved through a level very fast not to return there.

82
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:40,800
So, I mean, there's going to be times where the market just spikes up and spikes back down.

83
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:47,000
You know, usually that's due to, you know, some external factor. Maybe a stop got triggered.

84
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,700
It's not necessarily due to, you know, overall supply and demand.

85
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:55,000
So let's look at a chart here.

86
00:04:55,100 --> 00:04:56,200
This is a bond chart.

87
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:57,900
The market is going up.

88
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,100
It hits the high here at 746.

89
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:02,100
It starts to sell off.

90
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:05,076
But every time we sort of try to make a

91
00:05:05,076 --> 00:05:09,199
run back up at a high, I said we had been in an uptrend. We stop.

92
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:10,300
Okay, what's going to happen?

93
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:13,900
The market's either going to keep going higher, go sideways, or go lower.

94
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,120
So in this case, it starts going sideways to lower,

95
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:21,800
and each bar tries to go a little bit higher, but you get less volume.

96
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:24,100
You're getting single prints at the tops of these bars.

97
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:25,700
It's rejecting...

98
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:35,200
The prices up here above 160.17, 160.16 is rejecting these prices and the market sells off.

99
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:39,290
So the power of order flow is that it can

100
00:05:39,290 --> 00:05:41,799
show you more than just a price level where price was rejected.

101
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:45,500
It shows you how price is being rejected.

102
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:50,100
So one of the best signs of price rejection is when you

103
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:54,000
circle back to a price again, but on lesser volume the second time.

104
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:56,500
What do I mean by that?

105
00:05:57,300 --> 00:06:01,700
Say you have a level that the market is... looks like it's probably rejecting.

106
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:06,212
If we come back to that level and it's not showing any

107
00:06:06,212 --> 00:06:09,153
signs of acceptance and showing rejection, then chances

108
00:06:09,153 --> 00:06:13,198
are the price is going to go in the opposite direction.

109
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:14,500
So here's an example.

110
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:17,500
This is the ES, the E-minis chart.

111
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:19,700
We hit a high here at 836.

112
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:21,800
We got up to around the 1886 levels.

113
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:24,800
Came off four or five points.

114
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:27,900
And we try to make a run back up.

115
00:06:27,900 --> 00:06:31,200
You know, you got a green candle starting to run back up, but price is being rejected here.

116
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:34,700
You have a single print, you know, you have the ratio and market sells off.

117
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:38,400
Okay, it sort of stops. And again, you know, people want to try and take it higher.

118
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:43,100
But again, it stopped here with this, at this level with a single print.

119
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:48,400
It's sort of circling around to this area where you have the price rejection the first time.

120
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:49,500
It does get a little bit through.

121
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:51,113
So, you know, at this time, the

122
00:06:51,113 --> 00:06:52,265
second time it is trying

123
00:06:52,265 --> 00:06:54,799
to get, you know, a little bit higher and it does.

124
00:06:55,200 --> 00:07:00,300
But, you know, buyers are rejecting these levels above 1883.

125
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:04,400
There's no more follow-through, and the market sells off.

126
00:07:08,384 --> 00:07:12,170
Now, a common way to trade price rejection is to trade it

127
00:07:12,170 --> 00:07:14,694
to a level of price acceptance, which basically

128
00:07:14,694 --> 00:07:17,218
translates to you get into the trade around

129
00:07:17,218 --> 00:07:22,582
an area where there's price rejection and you enter it in an area where there's price acceptance.

130
00:07:23,784 --> 00:07:26,884
While there's nothing wrong with that approach, it does leave money on the table.

131
00:07:27,384 --> 00:07:32,584
I think sometimes it's hard to determine where price acceptance is.

132
00:07:33,584 --> 00:07:36,784
It's easier to determine price rejection.

133
00:07:38,284 --> 00:07:43,484
But order flows will allow you to trade from one price rejection to another.

134
00:07:44,684 --> 00:07:46,684
So from an area, rather than...

135
00:07:47,084 --> 00:07:50,897
Going from an area where price is being rejected to an area

136
00:07:50,897 --> 00:07:53,439
where price is being accepted, it might be

137
00:07:53,439 --> 00:07:55,981
more prudent to go from an area where

138
00:07:55,981 --> 00:08:01,383
price is being rejected in one direction versus where price is being rejected in the opposite direction.

139
00:08:03,284 --> 00:08:06,484
Because as the market moves up or down, it'll go from a high,

140
00:08:08,384 --> 00:08:09,907
at the top to the middle

141
00:08:09,907 --> 00:08:11,430
area where price is going to

142
00:08:11,430 --> 00:08:14,983
be accepted and then go down to an area where price is being rejected.

143
00:08:14,984 --> 00:08:17,206
Wouldn't you rather trade from the high to

144
00:08:17,206 --> 00:08:19,428
the low rather from the high to the

145
00:08:19,428 --> 00:08:24,983
middle of the range and then try and trade it from the middle of the range down to the low?

146
00:08:24,984 --> 00:08:26,884
It's just easier to trade from the high to the low.

147
00:08:27,684 --> 00:08:28,984
That's where you're going to make a lot of money.

148
00:08:30,484 --> 00:08:35,884
So remember, if price was accepted at a certain level, it would trade longer around there. move beyond that level.

149
00:08:36,384 --> 00:08:38,648
By reading the order flow, you can see if the

150
00:08:38,648 --> 00:08:40,459
market is rejecting that price because there won't

151
00:08:40,459 --> 00:08:44,082
be a flow of orders coming into the market to move the market beyond that price.

152
00:08:46,384 --> 00:08:50,684
Perfect example here is a crude order chart, crude oil chart.

153
00:08:50,884 --> 00:08:53,584
The market moved up, we hit a high, but price rejected up there.

154
00:08:54,884 --> 00:08:56,584
Where's the follow through as you're going higher?

155
00:08:56,984 --> 00:08:59,384
You've got three lots traded right on

156
00:08:59,384 --> 00:09:01,098
the high, and buyers are

157
00:09:01,098 --> 00:09:04,183
rejecting these higher prices. There's no more buying interest.

158
00:09:04,584 --> 00:09:05,684
Market starts to come off.

159
00:09:06,084 --> 00:09:08,484
Again, it tries to poke back higher.

160
00:09:08,484 --> 00:09:09,784
Again, it's rejecting price.

161
00:09:09,784 --> 00:09:11,884
You can see here in the single print of two lots.

162
00:09:12,984 --> 00:09:17,484
There's no more follow-throughs rejecting prices above 34.14, and the market sells off.

163
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:24,985
Now, when the market is underpriced or overpriced, the institutional traders generally

164
00:09:24,985 --> 00:09:27,481
will come in and buy or sell to

165
00:09:27,481 --> 00:09:31,538
bring back the market to a price level where more trade can occur.

166
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:34,440
So what are the signs of price rejection?

167
00:09:34,840 --> 00:09:36,640
Well, there's basically two signs.

168
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:43,940
And at first glance, you'll think that they're complete opposites, but you have to approach it in the same way.

169
00:09:44,040 --> 00:09:48,840
It can be low volume at edges of a bar or heavy volume at edges of a bar.

170
00:09:51,132 --> 00:09:54,173
Low volume at edges of an up bar occur because

171
00:09:54,173 --> 00:09:58,431
there's no longer any more buyers to sustain the move. The market is overvalued.

172
00:09:59,732 --> 00:10:03,065
Low volume at the bottom of a down bar occurs

173
00:10:03,065 --> 00:10:06,731
when there's no longer any more interest from sellers in selling.

174
00:10:07,432 --> 00:10:08,832
So, I mean, think about that.

175
00:10:09,432 --> 00:10:16,432
If a market is on the way up, but there's no more buying interest, what's happening.

176
00:10:17,232 --> 00:10:19,532
Buyers are thinking the market is too rich.

177
00:10:20,032 --> 00:10:24,365
And conversely, if the market is going lower, what's going

178
00:10:24,365 --> 00:10:26,965
to cause price to turn is

179
00:10:26,965 --> 00:10:31,731
going to be that there's no more sellers in the market.

180
00:10:33,932 --> 00:10:39,932
Yeah, technically that isn't necessarily price rejection, but if there's no more sellers,

181
00:10:40,832 --> 00:10:43,623
In the market, people that are selling the market are

182
00:10:43,623 --> 00:10:47,531
thinking there's no more capacity for them to make any money by selling there.

183
00:10:47,832 --> 00:10:49,846
So if they stop selling, they're rejecting those

184
00:10:49,846 --> 00:10:51,860
low prices and the market should bounce up

185
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:54,630
to a level where they could be happy to sell again.

186
00:10:55,532 --> 00:10:57,332
So here's a five-year chart.

187
00:10:59,132 --> 00:11:02,532
And, you know, you've got a couple things going on in here.

188
00:11:04,332 --> 00:11:07,632
You have down here at 812, you've got a single print.

189
00:11:07,932 --> 00:11:10,598
The market was working its way down, and

190
00:11:10,598 --> 00:11:12,598
sellers were rejecting this area in

191
00:11:12,598 --> 00:11:14,931
here. They weren't interested in selling anymore.

192
00:11:15,632 --> 00:11:19,232
In this case, buyers came in. You got a few buying imbalances, and the market trades up.

193
00:11:19,232 --> 00:11:22,232
to again this level up here

194
00:11:22,232 --> 00:11:26,232
where buyers have now sort of changed their

195
00:11:26,232 --> 00:11:28,732
thoughts that above 20 and a

196
00:11:28,732 --> 00:11:32,232
half, they think it's probably too expensive.

197
00:11:33,532 --> 00:11:34,732
And the market starts to come off.

198
00:11:34,932 --> 00:11:36,852
Maybe the buyers are stepping in again thinking,

199
00:11:36,852 --> 00:11:40,932
hey, let's make another run up at this high and see if we can take it higher.

200
00:11:41,332 --> 00:11:43,032
But, you know, they can't follow through.

201
00:11:44,232 --> 00:11:45,721
And, you know, what I was

202
00:11:45,721 --> 00:11:47,707
talking about earlier, rather than buying from an

203
00:11:47,707 --> 00:11:49,693
area down, you know, at the low to

204
00:11:49,693 --> 00:11:51,679
a level of price acceptance, you know, somewhere

205
00:11:51,679 --> 00:11:53,665
in here, you trade it from, you know,

206
00:11:53,665 --> 00:11:58,630
an area of price rejection to the next area of price rejection down to the next area of price rejection.

207
00:12:01,132 --> 00:12:05,532
It's one of those things where rather than necessarily leave money on

208
00:12:05,532 --> 00:12:08,465
the table, you can trade it from rejection

209
00:12:08,465 --> 00:12:12,131
to rejection if everything sort of sets up that way.

210
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:19,060
So heavy volume occurring at the edges of a bar are also a sign of rejection.

211
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:24,660
Even though that doesn't make sense, most people feel that heavy volume would be a level of acceptance.

212
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:27,142
But when the market cannot get through, get

213
00:12:27,142 --> 00:12:28,628
past a level and turns, one

214
00:12:28,628 --> 00:12:30,858
side of the market has thrown in the towel.

215
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:31,460
They gave up.

216
00:12:31,560 --> 00:12:32,760
They rejected the market.

217
00:12:33,260 --> 00:12:34,660
They rejected those prices.

218
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:38,860
Heavy volume occurs at the bottom of a move down.

219
00:12:39,560 --> 00:12:41,540
Sellers were met with strong passive buying and

220
00:12:41,540 --> 00:12:43,520
could not get the market to move past

221
00:12:43,520 --> 00:12:44,758
that level and gave up.

222
00:12:46,360 --> 00:12:49,026
In the opposite way of thinking, when the heavy volume

223
00:12:49,026 --> 00:12:51,692
occurs at the top of an up move, buyers were

224
00:12:51,692 --> 00:12:55,958
met with strong passive selling and could not get the market to move up any further.

225
00:12:56,260 --> 00:12:58,060
Until all the buyers gave up.

226
00:12:59,060 --> 00:13:04,160
What that looks like on an order flow chart is going to be often heavy volume.

227
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:11,160
Now people say that's trap traders, but you've got to realize what it is.

228
00:13:11,260 --> 00:13:14,560
The market is rallying, and there's heavy volume here.

229
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:18,960
People cannot get the market past this level here at 1910.

230
00:13:20,660 --> 00:13:24,860
Eventually they give up in trying to get a pass there, and they capitulate and they get out.

231
00:13:25,460 --> 00:13:27,960
You want to consider these people trapped? Yeah, you can.

232
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:30,460
They most likely got out pretty quick.

233
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:37,860
I hate to use the term trap traders because when you're trapped in something, it's really difficult to get out.

234
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:41,860
In the markets like E-minis, it's fairly easy to get out.

235
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:46,352
If you think you're trapped and you're getting

236
00:13:46,352 --> 00:13:49,344
out at three points from where you got

237
00:13:49,344 --> 00:13:53,458
in, I think there's a problem with the way you're trading.

238
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:56,394
